  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through Friday)\nIssued at 816 PM CDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nThe main impact of the long term period will be the hot and humid \nconditions. The upper level ridge remains entrenched over most of the\neastern US. As a result, afternoon highs are forecast to climb into\nthe low to mid 90s, which is 5 to 10 degrees above normal for this\ntime of year. Dewpoints should remain in the upper 60s to lower 70s\nkeeping max heat index values between 97-104 degrees. While this is\njust below advisory criteria, it will still be dangerously hot. For\nthose with outdoor plans next week make sure to practice good heat\nsafety and if possible limit outdoor exposure during the afternoon\nhours. The one change in the long term period forecast is that high\npressure is more centered over Alabama which would limit any \nafternoon convection. Hence, PoPs have decreased Tuesday and \nWednesday resulting in a dry forecast. There is still a low chance of\nafternoon showers and storms both Thursday and Friday, but given no \nsignificant forcing mechanism in place, coverage should be quite low.",
